  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nNegatively tilted upper trough entering the Western Plains will\nmove quickly through the area tonight and then off into the \nUpper Midwest. The surface low currently over central ND will \nlift off into southern Canada tonight, with northwest winds \nbehind the departing low tomorrow. Some of the guidance has \nwinds in southeastern ND approaching advisory criteria tonight \nand early Thursday morning. However, Bufkit soundings are very \ninconsistent and mixing is not ideal given overnight hours and \nnot very strong cold air advection. Winds could approach \nadvisory criteria, but not confident enough to include any \nheadlines for our area at this point. Another system comes out \nFriday into Saturday, again ejecting through the region fairly \nquickly. Northwest to near zonal flow sets up for Sunday into \nthe first part of next week, with temperatures dipping down to \nmore seasonable highs near the freezing mark. \n\n...Rain tonight and again Friday/Saturday...\n\nWith strong trough moving into the Plains, plenty of upper \nsupport for some precipitation, although most of the CAMs and \nlarger scale models have the strongest QPF signal north of us in\nCanada. Precipitation probabilities of at least some measurable\nrain near the Canadian border are quite high, but taper off \nquickly further south. Chances for at least a tenth of an inch \nof rain are around 60 percent in the far northwest part of the \nCWA, but zero elsewhere. The low moves quickly off to the \nnortheast, with another quick moving system coming through \nFriday into Saturday. Best chances for precipitation are again \nin the north, but high probabilities of at least a tenth of an \ninch of rain are more widespread during the second system, at \nleast north of the Highway 2 corridor.\n\n...Winter impact chances late Friday into Saturday morning...\n\nEnsemble members continue to trend to track further north with \nthe weekend low pressure system, with again much of the QPF \nending up in Canada instead of over our area. Temperatures for \neastern ND and western MN for these kind of tracks are also on \nthe warmer side. This would decrease our chances for impactful \nwinter precipitation, although the NBM continues to show 60 \npercent chances for 0.1 inch of snow around Towner and western \nCavalier counties by Saturday morning. Chances of over an inch \nfor that same area are less than 10 percent. Even if we do get \nsome accumulating snow, much of it will fall with borderline \ntemperatures, and likely be melting except on elevated surfaces \nand grassy areas. For now, confidence is not high enough to \ninclude any messaging of winter impacts, even minor ones.",
